## 1. INTRODUCTION
Future energy systems will still rely on the combustion of hydrocarbons, which generates electricity at the cost of emitting carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.1,2 Though prevailing technologies for carbon capture and storage (CCS) such as wet amine scrubbing have the potential for high rates of recovery of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ they are energy-intensive, which requires more than $30\%$ of the parasitic energy load of the power plant.3,4 The CCS energy efficiency could be improved if paired with low-energy porous solid-based separations that effectively capture and release $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from diverse flue gas streams. Numerous porous solids, including amine-functionalized silica5,6 and metal−organic frameworks (MOFs), have been widespread solutions for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture on a lab scale.7−9 However, they have not been broadly demonstrated in realistic conditions because of the relative scarcity of the microporous materials that are integrated into a scalable fluid contactor that permits low pressure drop, large sorption capacity, fast kinetics, and regeneration stability. Fiber sorbents provide the best combination of these four metrics, but to date, they have not been sufficiently effective for integrating high-performance porous solids such as MOFs.10
The insertion of alkylamines to open-metal sites within the pores of metal−organic frameworks with expanded lattices has been an effective strategy to enhance $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity at low partial pressures and stability under humid conditions.11,12 For instance, $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ $(\mathrm{dobpdc^{4-}}=4,4^{\prime}$ -dioxidobiphenyl$^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate) tethered with either diamines or tetraamines is one of the most highlighted porous materials that have exhibited exceptional capacity and stability.13,14 These MOFs feature a cooperative $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture mechanism achieved by continuous deprotonation and nucleophilic reactions between adjacent amine groups that rapidly create an ammonium carbamate chain reaction, thus enabling large $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake even at low partial pressures.13 Due to their steplike sorption behavior, these MOFs have a large $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ swing capacity and nearly perfect desorption during the temperature swing adsorption process.15 Recently, the insertion of a tetraamine, namely, $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -bis(3-aminopropyl)-1,4-diaminobutane, into the pores of the $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ was demonstrated with exceptional stability upon steam regeneration since the bulky tetraamine group strongly coordinates onto the metal sites in MOF.14 In diamine- $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ , one diamine molecule is attached to one open-metal site in the MOF. On the other hand, one tetraamine molecule is bound to two open-metal sites in tetraamine- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (Figure S1). This unique coordination and the use of less volatile tetraamine groups made it possible to increase the lifespan of the aminefunctionalized MOFs in practical conditions. Nevertheless, there is a gap between the lab and industry, which is only solvable by integrating these microporous materials into the scalable shapes that can fit into appropriate industrial separation devices. The fiber sorbent architecture provides a symmetrically open-porous polymer matrix with a homogeneously dispersed sorbent phase, usually up to $75\mathrm{-}80$ wt $\%$ sorbent loading. This unique structure reduces the average diffusion length of adsorbates compared to an inorganic monolith, ultimately resulting in improved separation efficiency (Figure S2).10,16 Scaled up synthesis of the fiber sorbents is achieved via the dry-wet spinning technique using water as a quench medium, making industrial-scale production possible. Sorbents such as zeolites and amine-impregnated silica with excellent water, chemical, and thermal and mechanical stabilities allow direct fiberspinning, and we shall refer to these fiber sorbents as Type 1 fiber sorbents.17,18 On the other hand, some MOF classes with open-metal sites that are unstable in water cannot be processed in spinning process.16 Amine tethering onto the open-metal sites enhances the water stability but is limited to humid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture conditions. The high-shear mixing and water quenching in the fiber spinning process exert severe mechanical stress to amine moieties.19 Instead of directly incorporating microporous materials into the fiber, the water-stable and mechanically rigid metal oxide precursor loaded fibers can be fabricated. Then the precursor-loaded fibers are converted into MOF fiber sorbents in the Type 2 route.
Here, we investigate the controlled synthesis of $\mathbf{Mg}.$ -based MOF nanocrystals within a polymeric fiber loaded with stable metal oxide precursors using the Type 2 route. A scalable MOF synthesis approach has been developed, where $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ ions are etched from the surface oxide and are incorporated into the MOF structure, enabling regulated growth of MOF nanocrystals.20 A mild removal of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}$ ions from the surface layers in a less-crowded pore space allowed a systematic and controlled dissolution-growth of MOF nanocrystals. We demonstrate the utility of this chemistry via in situ flow synthesis in a module packaged with precursor fibers, showing that the scalable production of MOF fiber sorbents is possible. The amine-appended $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ fiber sorbents discussed in this work selectively capture $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with productivities over 4 mmol per gram of the fiber, under humid flue gas conditions, at least five times higher than those of state-of-the-art aminefunctionalized silica-based fiber sorbents.21
## 2. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## 2.1. Control of MOF Growth in Fiber Sorbents
Figure 1a and b shows the synthesis route for MOF fiber sorbents in this work. Syringe tests produced small-scale fiber sorbent precursors $<10\mathrm{cm}$ in length) for the initial assessment of the fiber morphology and characterization of the materials before the full-scale fiber spinning ( $>100\mathrm{~m~}$ in total length). We investigated various dope solution compositions using $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ nanoparticles as seeds for MOF growth and poly(ether imide) (PEI) as a mesoporous polymeric support (Table S1). In an ideal fiber sorbent, nano- to microsized sorbent particles should be dispersed throughout the open-porous polymer matrix to minimize diffusion resistance so that the uptake equilibration time can be decreased, which further translates into the capability to build more productive, smaller separation devices. The effect of incorporating $\mathbf{MgO}$ nanoparticles with different loadings $(25-$ 73 wt $\%$ ) and particle sizes (50 and $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) on the openporous structure of the polymeric matrix was assessed. The dry weight loading of $\mathbf{MgO}$ of the formed $\mathrm{MgO/PEI}$ precursor fiber was calculated via thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) (Figure S3). As shown in Table S1, when the $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ content in the dope solution was approximately four times higher than that of the polymer, the maximum dry $\mathbf{MgO}$ loading of 73 wt $\%$ was achieved. A $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ loading over 73 wt $\%$ was deemed impractical because of the extremely high viscosity of the resultant solution, which hampers the solution processing via spinning technology.23 As shown in the cross-sectional scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images and energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) pattern of the formed fibers (Figures S4 and S5), open-porous structures with some macrovoids were obtained in all fibers except for the 73 wt $\%$ fiber. Additionally, minimal leaching of $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ nanoparticles (both 50 and $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) during the syringe test indicates that stable, large-scale spinning is possible (Table S1). The longchain linker of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dihydroxy-( $[1,1^{\prime}$ -biphenyl)- $^{\cdot3,3^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid (Figure S6) is only soluble in aprotic solvents such as dimethylformamide (DMF). Therefore, to proceed with further MOF synthesis within the fiber, reinforcement in chemical and physical stability of the poly(ether imide) was essential.24 Both thermal (i.e., heat treatment at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot$ ) and chemical (i.e., ethylenediamine and $p$ -xylylenediamine assisted) cross-linking was performed using the as-spun small-scale precursor fibers. The cross-linked precursor fibers were then converted into a $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{poly}\big($ (ether imide) fiber sorbent (hereafter, $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}\big)$ to elucidate the effect of crosslinking on MOF conversion. As shown in Figure $S7\mathsf{a}$ , the MOF conversion was low in the thermally cross-linked fiber indicated by the peak at $44^{\circ}$ of unreacted $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ remaining in the matrix,25 reflecting the kinetically limited diffusion of linker molecules in thermally shrunk fibers. On the other hand, chemically cross-linked precursor fibers showed nearly complete MOF conversion without damage to the fiber matrix (Figure S7b). Subsequently, MOF growth was optimized on the chemically cross-linked $\mathbf{MgO/PEI}$ precursor fibers by changing the molar concentration of the ligand solution, reaction temperature, and time. Figure S8a shows XRD patterns of the $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ synthesized using the different molar concentrations of the linker from 0.19 to $0.29{\mathrm{~M}}$ . The higher the ligand concentration, the higher the $\mathbf{MgO}$ etching rate, which promoted the MOF growth;20 however, when it exceeded $0.26\mathrm{M}$ , unreacted ligands showed up in the XRD pattern and these ligands recrystallized on the surface of the fiber (Figure S9). Similarly, the MOF crystallization rate was accelerated at high temperature, resulting in near-complete MOF conversion at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S8b). Figure $\operatorname{lc-g}$ shows SEM cross sections and XRD patterns of MOF fiber sorbents from $\mathrm{MgO/PEI}$ precursor fibers by controlling synthesis times at fixed reaction conditions of $0.26~\mathrm{M}$ and $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Spherical $\mathbf{MgO}$ precursors are converted into the rod-shaped $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ as the reaction proceeds. The inset images in Figure $\operatorname{lc-f}$ show a series of transformations from $\mathbf{MgO}$ to $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc). Within the first hour of the MOF conversion, $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ ions etched out from the $\mathbf{MgO}$ are nucleated into $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ across the $\mathbf{MgO}$ surfaces (Figure 1d and $\mathbf{g}$ ). The degree of $\mathbf{MgO}$ surface etching and the growth of MOF clusters are accelerated up to $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ of reaction, resulting in particles with urchin-like morphologies (Figure 1e). At $6\mathrm{{h}}$ , needle-shaped $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ crystals were fully grown along the 1D direction due to the anisotropic crystal structure (Figure 1f and g). The fiber samples were collected with different reaction times, and they were subjected to X-ray diffraction to determine the rate of $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ conversion to $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ . As shown in Figure ${\mathrm{1g,}}$ no significant change was observed after $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ of reaction. The relative intensity ratio between the (100) peak of $\mathrm{{\calMg}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ at $4.8^{\circ}$ and the unreacted $\mathbf{MgO}$ at $42.8^{\circ}$ increased to ${\sim}85000{:}1$ , after $6\mathrm{~h~}$ of reaction, which indicates that most of the $\mathbf{MgO}$ were converted into $\mathrm{{\calMg}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ (Figures $\mathsf{1g}$ and $S10\mathrm{a-c})$ . Furthermore, it was challenging to maintain the original morphology and the mechanical strength of the fiber sorbents over $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ of reaction due to the prolonged attack of the aprotic reaction mixture on the polymer phase (Figure S10d). SEM images and EDS of the optimal $\mathrm{Mg_{2}(d o b p d c)/P E I}$ fiber sorbents synthesized at $6\mathrm{~h~}$ demonstrated that the porous structure of the fiber was well maintained with the homogeneous MOF conversion (Figure S11). Thus, diamine and tetraamine are inserted into the optimized MOF fiber sorbents via a solvent-assisted impregnation method in batch reactors, and then the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity of the samples under $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at 1 bar (Figure 1h) is tested.
When MOFs are converted from $\mathbf{MgO}$ precursors in a fiber matrix, the dissolution step of $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ is essential because this step determines the MOF conversion time scale and the overall architecture of the fiber. An illustration explaining the controlled dissolution of $\mathbf{MgO}$ nanoparticles within the confined pore space is shown in Figure 2a. The synthetic approach breaks down to (i) dissolution on the surface of metal oxide, (ii) diffusion of metal ions and ligands, (iii) MOF nucleation and growth, and (iv) Ostwald ripening between $\mathrm{{\bf{Mg}O_{j}}}$ where the reaction rates at each stage are competitive.26 The metal oxides serve as a source for $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}$ . The mild synthesis solution with the linker controllably etches the $\mathbf{MgO}$ nanoparticles, and as the dissolution of $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ progresses, the MOF nucleation occurs with the supplied linker molecules. However, if the surface etching of $\hat{\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}$ occurs in a highly crowded pore space, with interparticle length below the critical length scale, partial aggregation between adjacent $\mathbf{MgO}$ particles is pronounced due to the Ostwald ripening.27 It is observed that when the $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ nanocrystals are subjected to solvothermal synthesis condition for less than $30\ \mathrm{min},$ , the dissolution of the $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ proceeds; however, not enough MOF crystals are nucleated or grown (Figure S16e). Within the first $30~\mathrm{min}$ of MOF conversion, highly crowded fiber precursors (e.g., $>71$ wt $\%$ loading of $\mathrm{MgO}_{\mathit{\Phi}_{\mathrm{.}}}^{\cdot}$ ) showed immediate Ostwald ripening between $\mathbf{MgO}$ nanoparticles that leads to the formation of partially converted MOF crystals (Figure $2\mathrm{b-d},$ ).
This has resulted in the formation of densified $\mathbf{MgO}$ due to the accelerated $\mathbf{MgO}$ agglomeration (Figures 2a and $S12\mathrm{d{-}f)}$ in fibers with $\mathbf{MgO}$ loading over 46 wt $\%$ . For high loadings 1 ${>}46$ wt $\%$ ), MOF growth occurred on the surface of the densified $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ layer or as a separate layer; therefore, the MOF conversion rate was reduced as the loading $\mathbf{MgO}$ increased under the same synthetic conditions (Figures S12d−f, S13, and S14). The effect of the critical interparticle length scale was more pronounced when the large-sized metal oxides $(300~\mathrm{nm})$ were used. Due to the formation of highly dense $\mathbf{MgO}$ layers in the crowded pore space, the resultant $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ did not maintain the original open-porous structure, and severe delamination of the MOF from the polymer layer was observed (Figures S12d−f and $S1S{\mathrm{a-h}}{\mathrm{.}}$ ).
For the controlled dissolution and subsequent MOF growth within the confined pore structures, the $\mathbf{MgO}$ loading was suppressed below 50 wt $\%$ in the precursor fiber. In this case, interparticle length exceeds the critical length scale, facilitating the MOF growth and minimizing the Ostwald ripening bewteen $\mathbf{MgO}$ nanoparticles. In the presence of the organic linker, the kinetics of $\mathbf{MgO}$ layer dissolution is coupled to MOF nanocrystal growth, resulting in the formation of uniform $\mathbf{Mg}$ -based MOF nanocrystals throughout the polymeric matrix (Figure $\scriptstyle2\mathsf{a},\mathsf{e}{-}\mathsf{g}$ and $\mathsf{S}12\mathsf{a}-\mathsf{c},$ ). Therefore, $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ synthesized with the 46 wt $\%$ precursor fibers with $50\ \mathrm{nm\MgO}$ maintained the original open-porous morphology of the polymeric matrix with nearly complete MOF conversion (Figures S13 and S15i−l). This synthetic approach represents an opportunity to reduce the MOF synthesis time (Figures $^{1\mathrm{g,h}}$ and S16) and also provides a method for the highly controlled synthesis of MOF nanocrystals within the confined space. The dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherms of the diamine $\cdot_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethylethylenediamine) and tetraamine $(N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3-aminopropyl)-1,4-diaminobutane) inserted $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PEI confirmed the successful synthesis of MOF nanocrystals in the fiber sorbent (Figure 1h). Even though the $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ from a 46 wt $\%$ precursor fiber using $50~\mathrm{\nm~\MgO}$ particles showed a slight loss in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity compared to highly loaded cases (Figure
S17−19), this fiber had excellent porous morphology for enhanced mass transfer and material processability for largescale module fabrication.
## 2.2. Full-Scale MOF Fiber Sorbent
Full-scale precursor fiber spinning was performed with a custom dry-jet wet-quench spinning apparatus (Figure S20 and Table S2) that produces fibers at $10{-}20\ \mathrm{m/min}$ . Figure 3a shows the $50\mathrm{\m\MgO/PEI}$ precursor fiber bundle fabricated in this work. With these full-scale precursor fibers, we performed MOF conversion in two different reactor setups to systematically understand the scaling-up process of MOF fiber sorbents: (1) batch synthesis and (2) in situ flow synthesis (Figure S21) for scalable MOF conversion. The precursor fibers had excellent flexibility, but for MOF converted fibers only a slight bend was allowed before destruction because the pores in the polymeric matrix were filled with MOF particles. $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PEI synthesized from the batch reactors also had excellent porous structures (Figure 3b and c). The $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\bar{\mathrm{PEI}}$ with a hollow and monolithic structure showed a slight increment in the outer diameter from 607 to $880~\mu\mathrm{m}$ and from 650 to $920\ \mu\mathrm{m},$ respectively, after MOF conversion from $\mathrm{MgO/PEI}$ precursor fibers. Batch-synthesized $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ was further functionalized with diamine and tetraamine moieties in the same batch reactor setup. Figure 3d and e shows the dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherms for diamine- and tetraamine- $\mathrm{\cdotMg_{2}(d o b p d c)/P E I}$ under different operating temperatures from 25 to $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Steplike sorption behavior (Figure S22) was also confirmed in the amine-appended MOF fiber sorbents, which corresponds to the cooperative $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture mechanism that translated into a high sorption capacity at low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ partial pressures. Different working capacities over specific temperature ranges (i.e., 25−100, $25-$ 125, $25\mathrm{-}150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\rangle$ and pressure (i.e., 150 and 1013 mbar) are shown in Table S3. The isosteric heats of sorption (Figure S23) calculated from the isotherms also validate the chemisorptive nature $(-Q_{s t}=71\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ in diamine MOF fiber sorbents and $66{-}100\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ in tetraamine MOF fiber sorbents) of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption process in amine-appended MOF fiber sorbents.13,14
The sorption kinetics (Figure 3f) of the MOF fiber sorbents were measured in a pressure-decay sorption apparatus using coal flue gas composition $\left(15\mathrm{mol}\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right.$ and balance $\mathbf{N}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) at 1 bar. For the diamine- $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI},$ , the time to reach the complete equilibrium was shorter than that of the MOF powder case due to the enhanced bulk mass transfer by the open-porous matrix of the MOF fiber sorbents. The mercury porosimetry (Figure S24) showed only a slight reduction of macroporosity in $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ than that of the precursor fiber, suggesting that the cross-linking of the polymeric matrix effectively enhanced the chemical stability of the fiber while maintaining the overall porosity. Interestingly, the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PEI fabricated from the highly loaded precursor (73 wt $\%$ ) exhibited a drastically decreased pore volume in the macropore range $(0.1-1\mu\mathrm{m})$ , which verified that the densification of $\mathbf{MgO}$ deteriorated the gas transport of the resultant MOF fiber sorbents (Figures 3f and S24). Amineappended $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ fabricated from 46 wt $9\%$ precursor fiber with $50\ \mathrm{nm\MgO}$ had a reduced BET surface area (up to $90\%$ ) after amine functionalization, indicating the effective insertion of amine groups into the micropores of MOF within the fiber sorbent (Figure S25).
On top of these, we demonstrated the in situ flow synthesis of MOF fiber sorbents in a modular device (Figure 4a). Initially, $\mathbf{MgO/PEI}$ precursor fibers were packaged in a $1/4^{\mathfrak{n}}$ stainless-steel module, which simultaneously served as a reactor for flow synthesis of MOF fiber sorbent, including polymer cross-linking and amine insertion steps, and a separation device that could be directly used for cyclic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption upon activation. The single-module approach minimizes the handling steps in the synthesis of porous materials as well as in making the separation device. Further, it prevents the MOF fiber sorbents from being directly exposed to moist air or another unwanted atmosphere during the fabrication/synthesis/testing process. Pimentel et al. originally demonstrated a similar approach with $\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ loaded fibers to fabricate HKUST-1 or with ZIF-8 loaded fiber sorbents using relatively benign solvents such as ethanol and water.28 Figure 4a shows the module used in this study, which depicts how the monolithic $\mathbf{MgO/PEI}$ precursor fibers are loaded in the module and further converted to amine-appended MOF fiber sorbents. Flow reaction used in cross-linking, MOF synthesis, and amine insertion could maximize the reaction extent at each step and reduce the overall synthesis cost by recycling the unreacted ligands and solvents.29 Especially, the flow synthesized fibers showed a higher degree of cross-linking than the batch synthesized fibers as probed by Fouriertransform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy and elemental analysis (Figure S26 and Table S4), which was advantageous in producing a more mechanically stable MOF fiber sorbent. With the optimized flow synthesis protocols (see Syntheses in the Supporting Information), modules containing multiple strands of diamine- and tetraamine- $\cdot\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{\ensuremath{g}}_{2}}$ (dobpdc)/PEI were fabricated and tested in a custom-built breakthrough system (Figure S27).
## 2.3. ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ Sorption at Flue Gas Compositions
Figure S28 compares the pressure drop as a function of superficial velocity for modules containing different amounts of diamine- $\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PEI fibers, which is a critical design factor in the fluid contactor showing the ability to process high gas flow rates. The void fraction and corresponding dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ breakthrough curves were monitored (Figure S29). In particular, carbon capture from various point sources requires a large volume of gases processed (typically $10^{6}$ scfm, standard cubic feet per minute);30 therefore, the fluid contactor with a lower pressure drop would greatly reduce the process cost. Since the density of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobjedc}\big)$ was 6 times lower than that of the $\mathbf{MgO}$ $\mathord{\sim}580$ and ${\sim}360\bar{0}~\mathrm{kg/m}^{3}$ , respectively),31 the final MOF fibers grew in size. As seen in Figures S28 and S29, the pressure drops increased as the number of loaded precursor fibers increased due to the reduced void fraction in the MOF converted module. For example, when more than 12 fibers were loaded into a $1/4^{\prime\prime}$ module with a cross-sectional area of $1.17\times10^{-5}\mathrm{m}^{2}.$ , deformation of the MOF fiber sorbent occurred during synthesis among adjacent fibers (Figures S29d and S30). The rapid increase in the pressure drop (Figure S28) was observed due to the lowest void fraction of $31\%$ in the module. The module fabricated with nine precursor fibers showed the lowest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ breakthrough capacity (Figure S29a) because sorbate bypassing occurred due to the large void fraction of $49\%$ . As the number of loaded precursor fibers increased up to 11 fibers, it was clear that the void space in the module diminished and the breakthrough capacity increased while the initial pressure drop was retained at increased superficial velocities. Therefore, the module fabricated with 11 precursor fibers ( $38\%$ void fraction) demonstrated optimal $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption performance, which was further tested at different feed conditions for multicycle $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption experiments (Figure S31).
Figure $4\mathsf{b{-e}}$ illustrates the five cyclic sorption−desorption breakthrough profiles of the diamine- and tetraamine$\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ module with $38\%$ void fraction (i.e., 11 fibers) for different flue gas conditions. Diamine$\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ showed impressive breakthrough capacity under a dry flue gas flow (Figure 4b), showing the highest uptake in the first cycle of $3.58\ \mathrm{\mmol/g}$ fiber, and then stabilized at about $3.31\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ fiber after three cycles ( $8\%$ reduction at the fifth cycle). There was a slight loss in the breakthrough capacity over several thermal cycles because of the partial volatilization of the diamine functional group with low molecular weight.32 To further simulate realistic feed conditions, cyclic sorption was performed with the humid flue gas (Figure 4d and see Supporting Information for detailed humidification methods). For every humid breakthrough campaign, a prehumidification step was used to reflect realistic conditions. The humid breakthrough capacity of the diamine$\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ was increased to $4.41\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ fiber due to the formation of bicarbonate species under the presence of water, which increased amine efficiency.33 However, after the first round of the humidity experiment, a $7\%$ reduction in breakthrough capacity was observed due to the sharpening of the breakthrough curve.
The humid breakthrough capacity was stabilized after three cycles to $3.8\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ fiber ( $14\%$ reduction in the fifth cycle). The relatively large loss in the sorption capacity of the diamine- $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ might be due to the high regeneration condition of 120 degrees (Table S3).
A multicyclic sorption test of tetraamine- $\mathrm{\cdotMg_{2}(d o b p d c)/P E I}$ was performed under the same conditions. The breakthrough capacities of the tetraamine- $\mathrm{\cdotMg_{2}(d o b p d c)/P E I}$ in the first cycle were 2.49 and $4.12\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ fiber in dry and humid conditions (Figure $\scriptstyle4c$ and $\mathbf{e}$ ), respectively, with the result that the breakthrough $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities of the tetraamine$\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ in flue gas composition were lower than those of diamine. Moreover, the equilibrium time of the tetraamine- $\mathrm{\cdotMg_{2}(d o b p d c)/P E I}$ was longer than that of the diamine because the tetraamine, which is bulkier than diamine, caused steric hindrance between neighboring ammonium carbamate chains formed in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.14 However, the strong interaction between metal-tetraamine or adjacent to the amines maintained humid breakthrough capacity up to $92\%$ after five cycles (Figure 4e). Since the humid feed stream desorbed during regeneration suppresses urea formation, the humid cyclic stability of tetraamine- $\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PEI was better than dry cyclic stability.34 In addition, long-term cyclic (up to 200 cycles) stabilities of diamine-/tetraamine$\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ in humid flue gas composition were measured (Figure 5). Diamine- $\mathrm{\cdotMg_{2}(d o b p d c)/P E I}$ showed up to $17\%$ loss in the sorption capacity after 200 cycles and tetreaamine- $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ showed only $9\%$ loss in the sorption capacity after 200 cycles of continued sorptiondesorption of humid flue gas stream. These results support the excellent cyclic stability of the MOF fiber sorbents in modular devices.
## 2.4. ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ Sorption at Direct Air Compositions
For negative emission strategies in CCS, the direct air capture (DAC) of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ could reduce the net atmospheric greenhouse gas concentration, providing that economical and energyefficient devices can be developed and deployed on a large scale. With our “process-ready” module packed with diamineand tetraamine- $\bar{\mathbf{M}}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PEI, we further tested the carbon capture performance of these modules in DAC conditions (Figure 6). The multistep $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherm of amine-appended $\mathrm{{\bfMg}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ affects the sorbate transport during breakthrough measurement, which is more pronounced under ultradilute concentrations in DAC conditions (i.e., 400 ppm of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ) than flue gas concentrations.35 In the breakthrough curves, a shock wave occurs when the slope of the isotherm is steep, whereas a dispersive wave occurs when the slope is gradual.35 The amine-appended $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PEI shows the multiple shock waves consisting of the shock and dispersive waves.35 The overall breakthrough time in which the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration fell less than $5\%$ of its inlet concentration, and the final equilibrium time was much longer than those of the flue gas case because the mass transfer rate of the aminefunctionalized MOF fiber sorbent was reduced by cooperative insertion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at lower concentrations.35 Diamine- and tetraamine- $\cdot\mathrm{M}_{\mathbf{\ensuremath{g}}_{2}}$ (dobpdc)/PEI exhibited dry breakthrough DAC capacities of 0.70 and $1.99\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ fiber, respectively, at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $400~\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 6).
## 3. CONCLUSIONS
Our results demonstrate that the microporous materials with exceptional $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities and stabilities can be solutionprocessed in a scalable shape (i.e., Type 2 fiber sorbent).16
Minimized handling steps in the single contactor module approach preserved the original excellent adsorptive properties of the MOF, passing those down to the scaled-up version in fiber shape. It is worth noting that the dry and humid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of the diamine- and tetraamine- $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ at flue gas composition have surpassed previously reported cases of fiber sorbents (Table S7).14,17,23,28,36,37 In our strategy for synthesizing MOFs in the polymeric fiber, recycling of the unreacted ligand or unused solvent through the flow chemistry devices is possible. This approach can greatly reduce the production cost of the MOF-based sorbents. Das et al. has demonstrated a $45\%$ reduction in production cost for MOF-74 with a recycled solvent mixture via a vapor-assisted dry-gel method.29 In addition, Sinha et al. estimated the energy and cost of mmen- $\cdot\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) and MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ -PEI-800 coated monoliths for DAC using the temperature-vacuum swing adsorption (TVSA) process.38 Both cases showed reasonable carbon capture prices $\$60-190$ per ton of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ captured). Despite the need for additional studies for the practical application of fiber sorbents, we believe that the flowsynthesized modular fiber sorbent system is a promising platform to demonstrate the cost of less than $\$100$ per ton of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ captured.38 The current integrative approach shows the opportunities for “lab” to “industry” in the carbon capture field. Our single module approach demonstrated that the designed sorbents, which have different microporosities and diverse functionalities, can be integrated into a rationally designed contactor to advance conventional approaches in CCS as well as uniform reaction of the sorbents in the contactor. These “small footprint” fiber sorbent modules would open the door for the use of the versatile “unspinnable” MOF materials that exhibit tremendous adsorption capacities but have not moved beyond powder-scale experiments.
## 4. EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Details of the full materials and experimental methods are explained in the Supporting Information.
## 4.1. Synthesis of the Amine-Mg2(dobpdc)/Poly(ether imide) Fiber Sorbents
The synthesis of a 4,4-dihydroxy-(1,1-biphenyl)-3,3-dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\big(H_{4}\big(d o b p d c\big)\big)}$ was scaled up based on a previously reported procedure39 because the ligand is consumed in large amounts in large-scale fiber synthesis. To prevent the destruction of the polymeric matrix by the aprotic solvent during the MOF conversion, the $\mathrm{\mathbf{Mg}O}$ fiber precursors were chemically cross-linked with ethylenediamine and $p$ -xylylenediamine solution for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . In the MOF conversion steps, a mass of fiber equivalent to $1.36\mathrm{\mmol}$ of $\mathrm{{\bfMgO}}$ loaded into the polymer matrix is added to $0.25~\mathrm{M}$ ligand solution and reacted at 120 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a batch reactor or flow module system. The resulting $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{PEI}$ fiber sorbents were washed with DMF ( $20~\mathrm{mL}\bar{\times}$ 5) and methanol $\left(20~\mathrm{mL}\times5\right)$ and activated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight in a vacuum. Fully activated $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PEI fiber sorbents were soaked in diamine solution (containing $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethylethylenediamine: anhydrous hexane) for $^\mathrm{~4~h~}$ or tetraamine solution $\left(N,N^{\prime}{\cdot}\ b i s(3\cdot$ aminopropyl)-1,4-diaminobutane: anhydrous toluene) for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at 60 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The samples were washed with each reaction solvent $\left(3\times20~\mathrm{mL}\right)$ ) and methanol $\phantom{-}3\times20~\mathrm{mL})$ and dried at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ The resulting fiber sorbents were activated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ before evaluation of crystallinity, porosity, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption of the sample.
